---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '@components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import AcreageCalculator from '@components/AcreageCalculator.astro';
import AdSlot from '@components/AdSlot.astro';
import FAQ from '@components/FAQ.astro';
import AuthorBio from '@components/AuthorBio.astro';
import RelatedCalculators from '@components/RelatedCalculators.astro';
import EmbedCTA from '@components/EmbedCTA.astro';
import { acreageFAQ } from '@data/faqs/acreage';
import { getRelatedCalculators } from '@data/calculators';

const title = "Acreage Calculator — Land Area in Acres, Hectares & Sq Ft";
const description = "Free acreage calculator for any land shape. Compute lot size in acres, square feet, square meters, and hectares — rectangle, circle, triangle, trapezoid or irregular 4-sided.";

const related = getRelatedCalculators('acreage-calculator', 4).map(c => ({
  title: c.shortTitle + ' Calculator',
  href: `/${c.slug}/`,
  desc: c.description
}));

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "WebApplication",
    "name": "Acreage Calculator",
    "applicationCategory": "UtilitiesApplication",
    "operatingSystem": "Any (web-based)",
    "description": description,
    "url": "https://materialcalc.com/acreage-calculator/",
    "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" }
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "HowTo",
    "name": "How to calculate acreage",
    "step": [
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Measure the property", "text": "Get accurate side dimensions from a survey, deed, or careful measurement." },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Choose the shape", "text": "Rectangle, circle, triangle, trapezoid, or irregular four-sided lot." },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Calculate square footage", "text": "Apply the area formula for your shape (e.g. L × W for rectangle)." },
      { "@type": "HowToStep", "name": "Convert to acres", "text": "Divide square feet by 43,560 to get acres." }
    ]
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={`${title} | MaterialCalc`} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <div class="container content-wrapper">
    <Breadcrumb trail={[
      { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
      { label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators/' },
      { label: 'Volume & Measurement', href: '/calculators/#volume' },
      { label: 'Acreage Calculator' }
    ]} />

    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>Acreage Calculator</h1>
      <p class="intro">
        Calculate the exact size of any land area in acres, square feet, square meters, and hectares.
        Supports rectangles, circles, triangles, trapezoids, and irregular 4-sided lots — perfect
        for property surveys, real estate, farming, and zoning calculations.
      </p>
    </header>

    <AcreageCalculator />

    <article class="content">
      <h2>How to Calculate Acreage</h2>
      <p>
        Acreage is just area expressed in acres — the standard land unit in the United States. The
        calculation is two steps: first compute the area in square feet using the appropriate shape
        formula, then divide by 43,560 (the number of square feet in one acre). Our calculator handles
        the math automatically, but understanding the formula helps you verify results and handle
        unusual shapes.
      </p>

      <div class="formula">
        Acres = Square feet ÷ 43,560<br>
        Square meters = Square feet × 0.0929<br>
        Hectares = Acres × 0.4047
      </div>

      <p>
        The number 43,560 originates from historical English measurement: an acre was defined as
        a furlong by a chain (660 ft × 66 ft = 43,560 sq ft). This odd-looking conversion factor
        is exact and has been used in US land law since colonial times.
      </p>

      <h3>Worked example: a typical suburban lot</h3>
      <p>
        Suppose your property is 200 ft long × 145 ft wide (a typical mid-sized suburban lot):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Square feet:</strong> 200 × 145 = <strong>29,000 sq ft</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Acres:</strong> 29,000 ÷ 43,560 = <strong>0.666 acres</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Square meters:</strong> 29,000 × 0.0929 = <strong>2,694 m²</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Hectares:</strong> 0.666 × 0.4047 = <strong>0.269 ha</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">An acre isn't actually a defined shape</div>
        An acre is purely a measure of area, not shape. A 1-acre square is 209.08 × 209.08 ft.
        A 1-acre rectangle could be 43.56 × 1,000 ft, or 50 × 871 ft, or any other dimensions that
        multiply to 43,560. Property assessors care about the total area, not the shape.
      </div>

      <AdSlot label="AdSense in-content #1" slotId="ac-mid-1" />

      <h2>Calculating Different Property Shapes</h2>
      <p>
        Real properties rarely come as perfect rectangles. Here's how to handle the most common
        property shapes.
      </p>

      <h3>Rectangle (most lots)</h3>
      <p>
        Length × Width. Most suburban lots are roughly rectangular even if not perfectly so. For
        small deviations (under 5°), approximate with a rectangle using the average dimensions.
      </p>

      <h3>Circle (round properties, easements)</h3>
      <p>
        π × radius². Useful for circular easements, well-drilling exclusion zones, or unusual
        property shapes. A 100 ft radius circular property is 31,416 sq ft, about 0.72 acres.
      </p>

      <h3>Triangle (corner lots, irregular shapes)</h3>
      <p>
        (Base × Height) ÷ 2. For corner lots where two property lines form a triangle, measure
        the base of the triangle and the perpendicular height (not the slanted side). A triangle
        with 200 ft base and 150 ft height = 15,000 sq ft = 0.34 acres.
      </p>

      <h3>Trapezoid (lots with parallel front/back lines but different widths)</h3>
      <p>
        ((Width A + Width B) ÷ 2) × Length. Many rural and farm lots are trapezoid-shaped because
        they follow road frontage on one side. A lot 100 ft wide at the road, 200 ft wide at the
        back, and 300 ft deep: ((100 + 200) ÷ 2) × 300 = 45,000 sq ft = 1.03 acres.
      </p>

      <h3>Irregular four-sided (most rural lots)</h3>
      <p>
        For 4-sided lots where no angles are right angles, you can use Bretschneider's formula
        (an approximation) when you know just the four side lengths. Our calculator includes this
        option. For most accurate results on truly irregular property, use a county tax assessor
        plot or a professional survey.
      </p>

      <h3>Complex polygons</h3>
      <p>
        Properties with 5+ sides or curves require either: (1) breaking into multiple simpler shapes
        and summing the area, or (2) using GIS data from your county. Many counties publish parcel
        data online with accurate square footage already calculated.
      </p>

      <h2>How Big Is an Acre? Real-World Comparisons</h2>
      <p>
        It's surprisingly hard to picture an acre in your head. These comparisons help:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="ref-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Comparison</th>
              <th>Size in acres</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1 acre (the reference)</td><td>1.00 acre = 43,560 sq ft = 4,047 m²</td></tr>
            <tr><td>American football field (including end zones)</td><td>1.32 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>American football field (playing area only)</td><td>1.10 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>FIFA soccer pitch (standard)</td><td>1.76 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Olympic-size swimming pool</td><td>0.03 acres (1,320 sq ft)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tennis court (singles)</td><td>0.06 acres (2,808 sq ft)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Basketball court (NBA)</td><td>0.11 acres (4,700 sq ft)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Standard suburban lot (avg)</td><td>0.20-0.40 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Walmart Supercenter (average)</td><td>4.0 acres of building</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Average US farm field</td><td>40-80 acres per field</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Central Park, NYC</td><td>843 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1 square mile</td><td>640 acres</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Common Land Area Conversions</h2>
      <p>
        Quick reference for the most common unit conversions involving acres:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="ref-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>1 acre equals...</th>
              <th>Conversion factor</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Square feet</td><td>43,560 sq ft</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Square yards</td><td>4,840 sq yd</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Square meters</td><td>4,046.86 m²</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Hectares</td><td>0.4047 ha</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Square miles</td><td>0.001563 sq mi (1/640)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Square kilometers</td><td>0.004047 km²</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Square chains (historical)</td><td>10 sq chains</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Square rods (historical)</td><td>160 sq rd</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Common Property Sizes and Their Acreage</h2>
      <p>
        Quick lookup table for common lot sizes — useful for real estate, zoning, and farming planning:
      </p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="ref-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Lot size (rectangular approx)</th>
              <th>Square feet</th>
              <th>Acres</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>50 × 100 ft (small urban)</td><td>5,000 sq ft</td><td>0.115 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>60 × 120 ft (typical city lot)</td><td>7,200 sq ft</td><td>0.165 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>75 × 150 ft (suburban small)</td><td>11,250 sq ft</td><td>0.258 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>100 × 150 ft (1/3 acre)</td><td>15,000 sq ft</td><td>0.344 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>110 × 200 ft (1/2 acre)</td><td>22,000 sq ft</td><td>0.505 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>150 × 200 ft (3/4 acre)</td><td>30,000 sq ft</td><td>0.689 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>200 × 220 ft (1 acre)</td><td>44,000 sq ft</td><td>1.010 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>300 × 290 ft (2 acres)</td><td>87,000 sq ft</td><td>1.997 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>400 × 545 ft (5 acres)</td><td>218,000 sq ft</td><td>5.005 acres</td></tr>
            <tr><td>660 × 660 ft (10 acres)</td><td>435,600 sq ft</td><td>10.000 acres</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <AdSlot label="AdSense in-content #2" slotId="ac-mid-2" />

      <h2>How to Measure Land Accurately</h2>
      <p>
        DIY acreage calculation depends on how accurate your measurements are. Options ranked from
        most to least accurate:
      </p>

      <h3>1. Professional survey</h3>
      <p>
        For closings, legal disputes, or anything where accuracy matters: hire a licensed land surveyor.
        Cost: $400-1,500 for typical residential lot. The deliverable is a stamped survey accepted by
        title companies, banks, and courts. Use county GIS data for free if you just need approximate
        numbers.
      </p>

      <h3>2. County GIS / parcel data</h3>
      <p>
        Most US counties publish parcel maps online with measured boundaries. Search "[your county]
        property search" or "[county] GIS." Accuracy: ±2-5% typically. Free and fast. Best option
        for non-legal use cases.
      </p>

      <h3>3. GPS with mapping app</h3>
      <p>
        Apps like GPS Fields Area Measure, Google Earth Pro, or Land Calculator let you walk the
        property perimeter while GPS records corners. Accuracy: ±5-10% in good conditions. Free or
        low-cost. Good for rural properties without published parcels.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Tape measurement</h3>
      <p>
        Old school. Mark corners with stakes, run a 100-ft tape along each side. Accuracy depends
        on terrain — slopes and obstacles introduce error. Good for small lots; impractical for
        rural acreage.
      </p>

      <h3>5. Stride pacing</h3>
      <p>
        Less accurate but quick. Calibrate your stride length, count steps along each side.
        Accuracy: ±10-20%. Useful for rough estimates only — never for legal documents or transactions.
      </p>

      <h2>Why Acreage Matters</h2>
      <p>
        Knowing your exact acreage matters for several practical reasons:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Property tax assessment</strong> — taxes are often calculated per acre or per parcel area. Errors in recorded acreage cost real money.</li>
        <li><strong>Zoning compliance</strong> — minimum lot sizes, setback distances, and building permits all reference acreage.</li>
        <li><strong>Real estate transactions</strong> — listing accuracy affects pricing. A 0.95-acre lot listed as "1 acre" can lead to legal disputes.</li>
        <li><strong>Farming and agriculture</strong> — seed quantities, fertilizer rates, yield estimates, and crop insurance all calculate per acre.</li>
        <li><strong>Insurance</strong> — coverage rates and premium calculations often use property size.</li>
        <li><strong>Subdivision potential</strong> — determining how many lots you can subdivide depends on local minimum lot sizes vs total area.</li>
        <li><strong>Land conservation</strong> — agricultural easements, conservation programs, and tax benefits are typically per-acre based.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Hectares vs Acres: International Context</h2>
      <p>
        The acre is used in the US, UK (residual), and a few other former British colonies. Most of
        the world uses hectares. The conversion is straightforward but the visual difference matters:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>1 hectare = 2.471 acres</strong> — about 2.5× larger</li>
        <li><strong>1 hectare = 10,000 m²</strong> — a 100m × 100m square</li>
        <li><strong>1 hectare = 107,639 sq ft</strong></li>
        <li><strong>10 hectares = 24.71 acres</strong> — typical small Canadian farm</li>
        <li><strong>1 acre = 0.405 hectares</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For real estate work involving international buyers/sellers, always convert and disclose
        both units clearly. A "10-acre" property is "4.05 hectares" to a European buyer — they
        may not know acres at all.
      </p>
    </article>

    <FAQ items={acreageFAQ} />

    <AuthorBio />

    <RelatedCalculators items={related} />

    <EmbedCTA calculator="acreage" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .content-wrapper { max-width: var(--content-max); }
  .page-header { padding: var(--s-3) 0 var(--s-5); }
  .content > p { line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px; }
  .content > ul, .content > ol { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7; }
  .content > ul li, .content > ol li { margin-bottom: var(--s-2); }
  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: var(--s-4) 0; }
  .table-wrap .ref-table { min-width: 540px; }
</style>
